{:edb/db-before-t 2662 :edb/db-after-t 2663 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535975 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:01:15.350Z" 13194139535975 true] [17592186228301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377546 13194139535975 true] [17592186228301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377546 13194139535975 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377547 13194139535975 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377551 13194139535975 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377554 13194139535975 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186377561 13194139535975 true] [17592186229645 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535975 true] [17592186229645 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/3a03159ef7285c7cbb571d8870edce98452b197d178334b4b06277f05a38d876.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 true] [17592186229645 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/q-336222.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}C$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"steady-state-charges-for-sinusoidal-voltage-rc-circuits\"></a>" 13194139535975 false] [17592186229645 1145 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{9}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{125},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/3}+\\frac{9}{500} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(9-5{e}^{-50t/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{9}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139535975 true] [17592186377546 1034 "0.018-0.01e^{-50t/3}" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377546 1111 #uuid "fbf3043f-851e-5d50-a708-561aa0a33e87" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377548 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1089 "q-337112" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1100 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+7q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1113 #uuid "edfe18ab-e7be-583e-8765-080447dfaf9b" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377547 1145 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+7{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+7\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{35}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-7t}+\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-7t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{35}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{35}$ coulombs." 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377549 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377550 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1041 "field-1" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377549 13194139535975 true] [17592186377548 1110 #uuid "49afba33-7934-5422-aebf-9d750179f1bd" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377549 1034 "\\frac{3}{35}" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377549 1111 #uuid "27af37c6-3353-5335-ab27-81718c1bc09f" 13194139535975 true] 