(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}+\\frac{5}{72}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{5}{72}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{5}{72}$ coulombs." 13194139535975 true] [17592186377552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377553 13194139535975 true] [17592186377552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535975 true] [17592186377552 1041 "field-1" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377553 13194139535975 true] [17592186377552 1110 #uuid "348188d0-1a9e-5e59-9a5c-5b4b723bfd00" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377553 1034 "\\frac{5}{72}" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377553 1111 #uuid "8c686199-74bd-59f1-b87d-f41dd2b9c99b" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377555 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1089 "q-337122" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1100 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.06$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{100}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1113 #uuid "98591216-f2fb-53d6-90e1-e780d730c534" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377554 1145 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{100},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{19}{500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25t/3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.06$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.06)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25(0.06)/3}≈0.025,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377556 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377557 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377558 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377559 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377560 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1041 "selection" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377559 13194139535975 true] [17592186377555 1110 #uuid "cda45fd6-b384-5df8-9349-0b2426c23742" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377556 1034 "0.067" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377556 1111 #uuid "da0761db-ec87-5b97-b0cd-ca1ae447df99" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377557 1034 "0.029" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377557 1111 #uuid "ab15bb63-b856-5e9a-abfe-34bd89a6df41" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377558 1034 "0.048" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377558 1111 #uuid "5cc2e81d-89d4-5828-b785-6985de1258fa" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377559 1034 "0.025" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377559 1111 #uuid "1a526002-4349-5c49-a094-5c886a84d9a8" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377560 1034 "0.021" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377560 1111 #uuid "c8025273-3afb-5f76-85ef-d77104a1b6b0" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1017 #edb/ref 17592186377562 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1089 "q-337135" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/6c5ce500d937822081d819e2c02f5fabece926498dd196e65d64dbdaa1dcb0bc.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1113 #uuid "23410692-ee8d-52af-9eed-f0c04ce14d9b" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377561 1145 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{4}{125}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{3}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=4,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=4+3=7.\n$$" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377563 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377564 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377565 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377566 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186377567 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1041 "selection" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1104 #edb/ref 17592186377564 13194139535975 true] [17592186377562 1110 #uuid "25388900-7637-5209-9190-49f8a3fbe4ef" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377563 1034 "8" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377563 1111 #uuid "85fb63d4-5445-5d63-ab45-9c225861c63a" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377564 1034 "7" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377564 1111 #uuid "44837f77-aa0e-5dfa-a922-cde2033f5ad3" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377565 1034 "6" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535975 true] [17592186377565 1111 #uuid "b54692c3-22cc-59ab-b2ca-5f620d931518" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377566 1034 "0" 13194139535975 true] [17592186377566 1103 